WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.280
You understand the mosaic of culture about how

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:03.780
movies sort of affect everything.

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:05.370
Has it always been like that for you?

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.340
Yeah.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:13.230
I mean, most of my life, I've gotten a lot of my

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:19.770
markers from movies and behavioral things, you

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:25.530
know, whether it was recognizing that no one can

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:30.040
slap me across the face, you know, like Sidney

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.260
said, do you know what?

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:33.190
Did you just hit me?

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.620
Did you really just hit me across my face?

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.170
See, that comes from there?

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.540
That's where I got that from.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.030
Because I knew as soon as Sidney turned around

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.310
and looked at that man, that that was not

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.150
acceptable.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.820
It was not acceptable.

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:56.950
So movies have often given me insight as to how

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.350
other people may be feeling about things.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.660
Of course, we're talking about "In The Heat of the Night"

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.820
What I registered in that moment, too, was the

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.250
other actor that look of delight on his face when

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:07.570
Sidney slapped him back.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.300
I mean, that was a thrill to me, because for the

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:12.760
first time, I felt like Sidney wasn't in the world as a

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:13.750
black person by himself.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.870
For me, Sidney was instinctual.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:19.000
He was an instinctual actor.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:24.690
And when I watched him, all right, that's the only

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.930
time, exactly as you're saying, that it was like,

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.880
Okay, you're not the only one here.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:33.840
You see the guy behind you sees, and he just

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.570
his face said everything. Said good...

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.430
Somebody need to slap your behind back, you

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.140
know, cause, you know, and I have to say, I love all

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:47.050
those actors in that scene because they could not

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:49.890
have been an easy one to shoot.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.980
You brought up Sidney and, you know, I gotta ask

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.290
you what it was like, if you remember seeing him

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.230
win the Oscar. What was your memory is of that?

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.440
Yes, I remember my mother and my brother, and I

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.780
jumping up and down, just jumping up and down.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:09.400
It's like, Wow! Our guy won! The winner is Sidney Poitier

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:18.790
And it's because we love Sidney.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.260
It's just because we loved the actor.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:27.220
And then later on, I sort of understood the

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:33.640
weight of what he had done.  Because it is a long

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.620
journey to this moment.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:42.970
I am naturally indebted to countless numbers of people

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:45.400
But I didn't understand it at the time.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:47.350
I just knew we love Sidney.

